2024-03-27T19:57:57 |>
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> > > I think we should reduce the number of zlibs in userland. Otherwise
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> > > security fixing becomes a nightmare. I will have a look at Perl.
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> >
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> > If you go this route, note that perl also contains a complete bzip2
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> > implementation. Since there has been concern, over a recent change
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> > proposed to tar, to only care about what's in base, we might want to
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> > add libbzip2 to base and link the perl module against it... (to be
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> > considered post-release)
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |>
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> And how many days or hours before someone says I want bzip2 support in
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> the bootblocks also (without any consideration their proposal blows up
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> 50% of the media, and will require a very significant amount of
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> development/test process).
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |>
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> I don't know why we limited ourselves to netinet. We should bring back
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> netiso. People should be able to depend upon is to supply them with
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |> choice even when it is irrelevant.
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |I don't want to start the discussion whether we want to import bzip2
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |into base.
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |The patch to use base zlib in Perl is rather simple. I am building
2024-03-27T19:57:57 |a release with it.
